+# Decision: Concurrency Policy for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers use shared tablets that can be offline for up to 48 hours. A worker on one tablet may submit a visit, then a coordinator or a second worker on another tablet — having fetched the same visit before the first submission arrived — submits their own independent edit of the same visit. The two submissions arrive at the server without either worker knowing the other has edited.
+
+The record store must accept both submissions without discarding either, then make clear which version is the authoritative current state for review and export. The mechanism chosen here determines the submission envelope tablet-sync must send, the review interface review-export must present to coordinators, and the fraction of visits that are held out of the partner's Friday export.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **No submitted payload may be discarded.** Every worker's submission must be retained regardless of which version becomes the current state. This applies to both options and eliminates any approach that overwrites or deletes a losing submission.
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent** (major) — tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable.* A visit's fields are not independent. A species identification and its cover estimate travel together; an observer name and the date of observation belong to a field worker's complete record. The path from a worker's original submission to an exported value should be traceable without reconstruction.
+
+- **Risk of silent correctness error** (major) — tenet: *Do not silently discard a submitted edit.* The inverse risk of that tenet: combining two submissions automatically can silently produce a composite that no field worker ever recorded. Some field edits are scientifically correlated in ways that are not deducible from field names alone — correcting a species code and its cover estimate are related changes a merge rule comparing column names cannot detect. An error introduced here reaches the science partner's dataset.
+
+- **Coordinator review workload** (moderate) — tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator.* Two coordinators manage roughly 6,000 plot visits per season. Visits that cannot be exported without coordinator action add to a weekly deadline queue; a policy that creates more such visits increases the risk of backlog before Friday.
+
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (moderate) — merge rules for scientific field data are non-trivial to write, test, and keep current. Species taxonomy updates, changes to the observer-list schema, and new visit field types each become a maintenance event for a merge algorithm. A simpler mechanism stays correct as the field protocol evolves.
+
+- **Tablet submission metadata** (minor) — the envelope tablets must send and the state they must retain affects tablet-sync complexity. Differences between options here are real but secondary to the server-side tradeoffs.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission is accepted as a complete, immutable revision of the visit. The server creates a revision record with a server-generated ID and the ID of the revision the worker was editing from (their base). When two submissions share the same base revision, both are accepted and the visit is marked **conflicted**. A coordinator reviews the two complete revisions side by side and selects one as authoritative; the resolution is recorded with the coordinator's identity and the competing revision IDs.
+
+Tablets retain the revision_id returned by the server after each sync. On the next submission they send that ID as the base. No field-level diffing or change tracking is needed on the tablet or the server.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- No-discard gate: **passes** — both revisions are retained; resolution names a winner but does not delete the loser.
+- Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent: **strong** — the winning revision is a field worker's complete, unmodified snapshot; no fields from two different workers are silently combined.
+- Risk of silent correctness error: **strong** — there is no combining step. Any composite must be explicitly authored by a coordinator through a new submission.
+- Coordinator review workload: **partial** — every concurrent edit, including edits to unrelated fields, creates a conflict requiring coordinator review. The conflict rate depends on how often two workers edit the same visit; in practice this is expected to be infrequent, but when it does occur the coordinator must review a full competing pair even when the difference is a corrected observer name.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — no merge rules to write or maintain. Conflict detection is a single equality check on base revision IDs.
+- Tablet submission metadata: **strong** — tablets track one value (the latest revision_id) and include it in the submission envelope.
+
+*Summary: maximally preserves worker intent and scientific traceability; trades a larger conflict queue for the guarantee that no automated combination ever occurs.*
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission carries a base snapshot of the fields as the worker saw them and the set of fields they changed. The server computes a three-way merge: fields changed by only one submission are applied automatically; fields changed by both — including cases where the same field was edited to different values — are flagged for coordinator review. Visits with no overlapping changes are resolved automatically; only visits with genuine field-level conflicts require coordinator attention.
+
+Tablets retain the full base snapshot at the time of last sync and track which fields the worker has edited. The submission carries both the base snapshot and the change set.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- No-discard gate: **passes** — original submissions are retained; the merge result is a derived representation, not a replacement.
+- Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent: **partial** — automatically merged visits combine fields from two workers' submissions into a composite record that neither worker submitted. Most of the time this composite is correct, but the algorithm operates on field names and cannot detect scientific dependencies between fields (a species code change and its associated cover estimate, an observer correction paired with a date correction). A merge that combines them from separate submissions produces a record no worker recorded.
+- Risk of silent correctness error: **weak** — automatic combination of fields is the central mechanism. The merge algorithm does not have access to the domain knowledge required to identify all scientifically related field pairs. A silently merged record that reaches the science partner's CSV is not detectable without comparing back to the original submissions.
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong** — visits where two workers edited different fields are resolved without coordinator involvement. Only true field-level conflicts surface for review. This is a meaningful reduction in queue size at the cost of the correctness risk above.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — three-way merge rules must handle scalars, the observer list (order-independent set), and species entries (keyed by species code). As species taxonomy or the visit schema evolves, merge rules must be updated. Observer-list merges have no natural resolution rule when both workers added different observers; cover estimate merges require a decision rule (average? latest? flag?). Each rule is a maintained invariant.
+- Tablet submission metadata: **weak** — tablets must retain the full base snapshot at last sync, track which fields the worker edited, and transmit a structured change set. This is substantially more state and logic than a single revision ID.
+
+*Summary: reduces coordinator queue by resolving unambiguous merges automatically; introduces a maintained rule set and the possibility of scientifically incorrect automatic combinations reaching export.*
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on the intersection of the two major criteria: preservation of whole-visit scientific intent and the risk of silent correctness error. Both cut against field-aware three-way merge for the same underlying reason — the server has no access to the domain semantics needed to judge which field changes are scientifically related. The team's data ends up in a regional science partner's dataset; an automatically merged record that silently combines a species-code correction from one worker with the original cover estimate from another is a data quality error that is invisible at export time.
+
+The moderate coordinator workload advantage of three-way merge is real but does not outweigh this. The expected conflict rate is low — most visits are edited by one worker, and concurrent edits require two workers to independently fetch and edit the same visit within a sync window. At 6,000 visits per season and an expected concurrent-edit rate that is a small fraction of that, two coordinators can absorb the additional review without a staffing change.
+
+The maintenance burden criterion reinforces the selection: merge rules for a scientific dataset must be correct by domain, not just by field name. That is a standing obligation that grows as the field protocol evolves.
+
+**What this forecloses:** automatic resolution of concurrent edits, even when the changes are genuinely unrelated (e.g., two workers each correcting a typo in a different observer's name). Every concurrent edit at the same base creates a coordinator task.
+
+**What this obligates downstream:**
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain the revision_id returned by the server after each successful submission and include it as `base_revision_id` in subsequent submissions for the same visit. Local storage for a visit needs to retain this ID across restarts and multi-day disconnection. Tablet-sync must also be able to display to a field worker that their submitted revision is one of several competing versions awaiting coordinator review.
+- **Review-export** must present a side-by-side comparison of competing revisions for the coordinator's resolution workflow. It must surface the coordinator's resolution interface for conflicted visits, record the resolution (winning revision ID, coordinator identity, timestamp, competing revision IDs), and exclude conflicted visits from the export set until resolved. The coordinator needs enough context — both complete visit snapshots and their lineage — to make an informed selection.
