 # Record Store
 
-## Context
+## Context and Design Philosophy
 
 This node owns visit persistence and the meaning of a current visit when independently edited copies arrive. A visit is identified by a client-generated UUID and refers to a catalog plot ID. Updates can arrive days after their starting state was fetched. The server must not treat arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
 
-Tablet-sync and review-export are not yet designed. Their contracts depend on the concurrency mechanism selected here. Neither the HLD nor the catalog contract selects that mechanism.
+The concurrency policy is **whole-visit revisions**: each accepted worker submission is preserved as a complete, immutable revision of the visit. When two submissions diverge from the same starting revision, both are retained and the visit is marked conflicted for coordinator review. A coordinator resolves a conflict by selecting the authoritative revision. No submitted payload is ever discarded.
 
-## Architecture
+Tablet-sync and review-export are not yet designed. Their contracts depend on this node: tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry state machine around the submission envelope and returned revision IDs; review-export sizes its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the competing-revision model.
 
-The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
+## Revision Model
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+A **revision** is the server's durable record of one accepted submission's complete visit snapshot. It is immutable once created.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+| Field | Type | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| revision_id | UUID | Server-generated; returned to the submitter on acceptance |
+| visit_id | UUID | Client-generated; groups all revisions for the same visit |
+| base_revision_id | UUID or null | The revision the submitter was editing from; null for a visit's first submission |
+| submission_id | UUID | Foreign key into the preserved submission record |
+| created_at | timestamp | Server receipt time |
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+A revision is the **head** of its visit when no other accepted revision names it as its own base. Competing revisions arise when two accepted submissions share the same base_revision_id (including when both are null for the same visit_id).
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+## Visit States
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+A visit is in one of three states at any time:
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+- **unreviewed**: exactly one head revision; no competing siblings exist at the same base.
+- **conflicted**: two or more accepted revisions share the same base_revision_id (or both are null for the same visit_id), and no coordinator resolution covers them.
+- **resolved**: a coordinator has selected the authoritative revision. The resolution names the winning revision_id; the winning revision becomes the new head.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+State transitions:
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+```
+(new visit)
+      ↓ first submission accepted
+  unreviewed
+      ↓ competing submission accepted at the same base
+  conflicted
+      ↓ coordinator resolution recorded
+  resolved
+      ↓ further competing submissions against the resolved head
+  conflicted  (cycle resumes)
+```
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+A resolution does not delete or modify the losing revisions; they remain queryable for audit and provenance.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Submission Envelope
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+| Field | Notes |
+|---|---|
+| visit_id | Client-generated UUID identifying the visit |
+| idempotency_key | Client-generated; scoped to visit_id |
+| base_revision_id | The revision_id the worker last fetched; null for a new visit |
+| observation_date | Date of the field observation |
+| observer_list | List of worker identifiers |
+| species_cover_estimates | List of `{species_code, cover_pct}` entries |
+| notes | Free text |
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Before accepting a submission the server authenticates the submitting worker, validates field types, and confirms the plot reference exists in the site catalog.
+
+## Submission Processing
+
+On each incoming submission:
+
+1. **Idempotency check.** If the idempotency key is already accepted for this visit_id with an identical payload, return the original acceptance result. If the key is accepted with a different payload, reject.
+2. **Validation.** Authenticate the worker; validate field types and plot reference.
+3. **Conflict detection.** Look up accepted revisions for this visit_id that share the same base_revision_id (treating null as a matchable value). If any exist, create the new revision and mark the visit **conflicted**. Otherwise create the revision and mark the visit **unreviewed**.
+4. **Response.** Return the server-generated revision_id and the resulting visit state.
+
+## Resolution Operations
+
+A coordinator resolves a conflicted visit by recording a resolution that names the winning revision_id. The system:
+
+- Records the coordinator's identity, timestamp, and the IDs of the competing revisions considered.
+- Marks the visit **resolved**.
+- Treats the winning revision as the new head; subsequent submissions use its revision_id as their base.
+
+A coordinator who finds neither competing revision satisfactory may author a corrected visit by submitting it through the normal submission path (naming one of the competing revisions as base), then resolving the conflict in favor of the new submission.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+**Current-state retrieval** returns the head revision's fields when the visit is unreviewed or resolved. When the visit is conflicted it returns a conflict indicator and the list of competing revision IDs.
+
+**Submission history retrieval** returns all revisions for a visit in server receipt order, each with its base_revision_id, creating a readable lineage for coordinators and auditors.
+
+## Export Eligibility
+
+A visit is eligible for export when its state is **unreviewed** or **resolved**. Conflicted visits are held out of the export set until resolved. Review-export queries this state directly; it does not re-evaluate revision lineage.
+
+## API Operations
+
+| Operation | Description |
+|---|---|
+| POST /visits/{visit_id}/submissions | Accept a submission; return revision_id and resulting visit state |
+| GET /visits/{visit_id} | Return current-state fields and conflict indicator |
+| GET /visits/{visit_id}/revisions | Return full revision history with lineage |
+| POST /visits/{visit_id}/resolution | Record coordinator resolution; return updated visit state |
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | See [decisions/concurrency-policy.md](decisions/concurrency-policy.md). Whole-visit revisions preserve each worker's submitted visit as an unmodified unit; three-way merge risks silently combining scientifically related edits the algorithm cannot detect from field names alone. |
+| Revision ID generation | Server-generated UUID | Client-generated, timestamp-based | Server generation avoids ID collisions from offline clients without requiring coordination between tablets. The server returns the revision_id on acceptance; tablets store it as the base for subsequent edits. |
+| Resolutions are records, not mutations | Resolution record names winning revision_id | Delete or overwrite losing revisions | Original submissions must never be discarded (HLD tenet). Losing revisions remain queryable for provenance and audit. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+1. Maximum competing revisions before the system warns coordinators — product decision; no cap is implied by the current design.
+2. Whether the coordinator's synthetic-correction submission goes through the normal worker-authentication path or a separate coordinator-scoped path — raise when designing review-export.
+
